lead:
  - "A young child rarely walks in and says, I've been feeling anxious since the move. They don't have those words yet. Instead it comes out sideways: the stomachaches with no cause, the meltdown over the wrong-colored cup, the sudden clinging, the trouble at drop-off. A child's first language isn't conversation. It's behavior and play."
  - "So we don't sit a seven-year-old down for a talk. We meet them in the language they actually speak. Through play, drawing, and stories, a child will show you what they can't yet tell you, and slowly build the words for it. What looks like acting out is usually a kid saying something important in the only way they know how. Our job is to listen in their language, not demand they learn ours first."
